{
  "id": "find-the-duplicate-number-cyclic",
  "questionId": "find-the-duplicate-number-cyclic",
  "slug": "find-the-duplicate-number-cyclic",
  "title": "Find the Duplicate Number (Cyclic Sort)",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Cyclic sort",
  "patterns": [
    "cyclic sort",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if there were no duplicate, every value could be placed at index value-1 with no conflicts, exactly like the cyclic sort array problem. what happens when a duplicate exists?",
    "walk through with a pointer i. while nums[i] != i+1, look at where nums[i] wants to go (index nums[i]-1). if that slot already holds the same value, you've found your duplicate",
    "otherwise, swap nums[i] into its target slot and try again at i. every successful swap places a value correctly, so this still finishes in linear time",
    "the duplicate is the one value that can never find an empty home, since two copies both compete for the same index"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "implementing Floyd's cycle detection (tortoise and hare) instead of the requested cyclic-sort placement approach",
    "infinite looping by swapping a value with itself when nums[i] == nums[nums[i]-1] but i != nums[i]-1, without checking for that case first",
    "mutating the input in a way that loses track of which index revealed the conflict"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of n + 1 integers, where every value is in the\nrange `[1, n]`. That guarantees at least one value repeats, though it may\nrepeat more than twice, and there is exactly one such repeated value.\n\n**Find and return the duplicate value, solving it with the cyclic sort placement idea rather than Floyd's cycle detection.**\n\nPlace each element at its target index and watch for the conflict that reveals the duplicate.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "array has n+1 elements, values are in the range [1, n]",
      "exactly one value is duplicated, but it may appear more than twice",
      "do not modify... actually you may treat the array as freely mutable internally, but must return the correct duplicate value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "the duplicate value appears three or more times",
      "n = 1, array is [1, 1]",
      "the duplicate is the smallest or largest possible value in range"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
